.\"                                      Hey, vim: ft=nroff
.TH SCREENATOR 1 "October 29, 2026"
.SH NAME
Screenator \- vibe-coded screen annotation tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B screenator
.RI [ options ]
.br
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBScreenator\fP lets you draw annotations over all your running applications.
It can run in the background and be activated on demand. The drawing stays on
screen as long as you want, and you can continue to use your applications
while it is visible.
.br
\fBScreenator\fP is a fork of Gromit-MPX 1.4.2. It adds a small draggable
on-screen icon whose left/right click opens a menu with the drawing tools
(pen, marker, shapes, text and eraser) and the settings (line colour,
thickness, eraser size, opacity, shape fill, text font and floating icon
size). The same menu is available from the system tray icon.
.br
The settings indicator is a ring around the floating icon. The inner white
ring is divided into nine equal sectors, one for each line thickness (1, 2,
3, 4, 5, 6, 12, 24 and 48 pixels), each with a one pixel black stroke. Starting at
angle 0 and going counter clockwise, the sectors up to and including the
selected thickness are filled magenta. The outer six pixel ring has the
line colour in its upper half and the shape fill colour in its lower half; when
shapes are not filled, the lower half shows twelve black and white radial
stripes. Both halves have a one pixel black stroke. Left or right click on the
ring opens the same menu as the floating icon. "Show Settings Indicator" in
the menu turns it off and on; it is on by default and is disabled while the
floating icon is hidden.
.br
The Marker draws a highlighter stroke with a fixed 32 pixel width, using the
current line colour. Its opacity is 40% by default and is independent of the
global pen opacity. Long right click, or a long press of the stylus second side
button, opens the Marker Opacity menu with 10%, 20%, 30%, 40%, 50%, 60%, 70%,
80% and 90%; the choice is remembered across restarts. Selecting the Marker switches the line
colour to yellow; leaving the Marker or exiting painting restores the colour
that was active before. The mouse wheel or the stylus first side button changes
its colour.
.br
While painting mode is active, the mouse wheel changes the setting selected in
the Scroll Mode menu: Color steps the line colours, including the custom
colour, or the shape fill colours over the Paint Bucket; Thickness steps
through 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 12, 24 and 48 pixels. With the Eraser tool selected,
the wheel changes the eraser size instead. Long-press the right mouse button
to choose Color or Thickness; a short right click finishes the current shape
or exits painting like Escape. The change is the same as picking it from
the menu, so it is kept when another tool is selected and is shown in the
settings indicator and the menus.
.br
The Tool Activation Delay submenu can delay a newly selected tool by 0, 1, 2,
3, 4, 5, 7, 9 or 11 seconds. The delay is always 0 when the application starts
and is not remembered across restarts. During the delay the previously
selected tool stays active. If the pointer grab is blocked when the delay ends, for example
by another application's context menu, Screenator freezes the current screen
as a temporary background so the tool can draw over it; exiting the tool
removes the frozen background.
.br
The Shapes submenu contains the Marker, Line, Spline, Polygon, Triangle,
Arrow, Ellipse, Rounded Rectangle, Paint Bucket, Math Function and Parallel
Lines tools.
Shapes are drawn by dragging from the start point to the end point. The line
and the arrow follow the drag direction, which snaps to 0, 30, 45, 60, 90 and
so on while Ctrl is held; without it the direction follows the pointer. The
arrow adds a triangular head at the end point by default and stays easy to see
even with a thin line. A square handle offset from the middle of the line
changes the heads: pressing it resets to none and dragging away cycles start,
end and both. A placed line or arrow stays editable: each end point has a
round handle, and dragging the line itself moves the whole shape; clicking
outside, double-clicking or right-clicking commits it.
Ellipses, rounded rectangles, polygons and triangles can be filled using the
"Shapes Fill Color" submenu; the default is no fill and green is its first
colour. Their outlines are drawn outside the shape border, so a thick stroke
never covers the inside of the shape. Both the line colour and the shape fill
colour have a "Custom..." entry that opens a colour chooser; the custom colour
starts as magenta and is remembered.
.br
The "Screen Paper" submenu turns the screen into paper for drawing and
writing: None shows the normal desktop, Plain a white page, Ruled a white page
with blue writing lines and Graph a white page with a grey square grid. The
paper is drawn below the annotations and above the other windows, the floating
icon and its menu stay above it, it takes the mouse and keyboard so clicks and
typing do not reach the desktop or the applications below, and it stays in
place when the screen is cleared. While a paper is active, Ctrl+Z and
Ctrl+Shift+Z undo and redo, Ctrl+Q exits the paper, Ctrl+T shows or hides the
drawings and Alt+F4 quits.
.br
The Spline tool is placed click by click instead: every click adds a node, the
nodes are joined by a smooth Bezier curve, and dragging a node moves it. Every
node has two round Bezier handles; dragging one mirrors the other so the curve
stays smooth, and holding Ctrl while dragging moves only that side, which gives
the node a corner. Click the last node again, double-click or right-click to
finish the curve.
.br
The Polygon tool uses the first click as its centre; dragging then sets the
radius and rotation around that centre, while a plain click keeps a default
size. The shape stays editable with four handles: the square handle controls
the number of sides from 3 to 16 (pressing it resets the shape to 3 sides;
moving away from it in any direction adds sides and moving back towards it
removes them, down to 3 at the handle), one round handle sets the radius, one
sets the rotation, and one moves the inner vertices outwards until the shape
becomes a regular polygon; that handle snaps to the flat edge when it gets
close, so a regular polygon is easy to hit. Drag inside the shape to move it.
Clicking empty space commits it and places the next one; double-click or
right-click commits it.
.br
The Triangle tool places the next vertex with each click. A line follows the
pointer after the first click, and the remaining sides follow after the second
click. Dragging does not place a point; the line keeps following the pointer
until the next click. The third click fixes the last vertex and closes the
triangle. The triangle keeps three round vertex handles, so it
can be stretched and skewed freely; holding Ctrl while dragging a vertex
snaps the angle at that vertex to 30, 45, 60 or 90 degrees. Drag inside the
triangle to move the whole shape. Clicking empty space commits it and places
the next one, and a double-click or right-click commits it.
.br
The Ellipse and Rounded Rectangle stay editable after they have been dragged
out. Every placed shape keeps a square size handle on each of its four corners
and a round rotation handle above it, and dragging inside the shape moves it;
rotation is free, and holding Ctrl snaps it to 0, 30, 45, 60, 90 and so on.
The Rounded Rectangle also keeps a roundness handle on its top edge:
dragging it away from the corner rounds the corners more, and dragging it
towards the corner makes them sharper. Clicking outside the shape,
double-clicking or right-clicking commits it.
.br
The Math Function tool opens a dialog for the expression and the range. The
Rectangular mode plots y = f(x) over an x range and takes a function of x; the
Polar mode plots r = f(theta) over a theta range, where theta is reserved for
the angle and the default range is 0 to 2*pi, a full circle. "pi" and "e" are
the constants pi and Euler's number, and multiplication can be implied
("2pi*x"). A preset drop-down fills the expression with "cos(2*pi*x)",
"sin(2*pi*x)", "unit(x)", "delta(x)", "rect(x)", "e^(x)", "log(x)",
"1/x", "sin(2*pi*x)/(2*pi*x)" or "(cos(2*pi*x)+2)*cos(20*pi*x)"; in polar
mode the same presets use theta instead of x. unit(x) is the unit step,
delta(x) is the Dirac delta, drawn as an arrow at the point where its argument
is zero, and rect(x) is a rectangle pulse that is 1 for |x| <= 1/2 and 0
elsewhere. The defaults are "cos(2*pi*x)" from x = -2 to
x = 2 and "cos(3*theta)" from theta = 0 to 2*pi; each mode keeps its own
expression and range. The plot is drawn inside a frame that is dragged out
like the other shapes and is scaled to fill it, so the corner handles also set
its amplitude; polar plots use one scale for both axes, so the curve stays
round in a non-square frame. The plot can be moved, resized and rotated with the usual
handles. Selecting the tool from the Shapes menu asks for the expression
first, and choosing it again changes the expression; a plot that is still
editable updates at once. A point where the
expression is undefined is filled in with its limit when both sides agree, so
"sin(2*pi*x)/(2*pi*x)" is drawn through x = 0; a real pole such as "1/x" or "tan(x)"
breaks the curve instead.
.br
The Parallel Lines tool draws between 2 and 40 evenly spaced vertical lines
with the current line thickness. The square handle outside the left edge sets
their number: pressing it resets the shape to two lines and moving away adds
more. The square handle outside the right edge changes the arrowheads on the
lines: pressing it resets to none and moving away adds them in the order top,
bottom and both; the handle shows the current mode. The lines can be moved,
resized and rotated with the usual handles.
.br
The Random Signal tool draws a random waveform. A single click places a
default-size signal centred on the click; dragging out a frame sets its size.
The square handle outside the left edge resets the smoothness to 1 (sharpest);
dragging it away in any direction raises it up to 16 (smoothest). The default
is 5. Clicking the signal without dragging generates a new signal with a new
seed. The frame can be moved, resized and rotated with the usual handles.
.br
The Paint Bucket fills the closed area under the click with the Shapes Fill
Color. The area must be enclosed by drawn strokes and must not reach the screen
edge; otherwise nothing is filled, so clicking outside a shape cannot flood the
whole screen. With None selected the bucket erases the enclosed fill instead,
leaving the strokes around it. The tool shows a bucket cursor.
.br
The eraser size can be set to 20, 40, 60 or 120 pixels from the menu. The
erasing area and the circular eraser cursor both match the selected size.
.br
The Text tool lets you write directly on the screen. Click where the text
should start and type: a small borderless editor appears at that position,
using the pen colour and the font selected by "Change Font". The text is
centred vertically on the click point. The text and its blinking caret are
drawn above the drawings and the screen paper, so they stay visible over them.
The middle mouse button still erases while it is held, also with the Text tool
selected. Press Enter to place the text or Escape to cancel it. A placed text
stays editable: drag it to
move it, drag a corner handle to change the font size, and drag its round
handle to rotate it.
.br
The pen, font and floating icon position settings are remembered in
.I ~/.config/screenator.ini
and can be restored to their defaults with the "Reset Settings" menu entry.
The screen paper is not restored at startup; the normal desktop is always
shown.
.br
The "Settings" submenu holds "Save annotations on exit", "Change Font", "Show
Settings Indicator", "Floating Icon Position..." and "Reset Settings".
"Floating Icon Position..." opens a dialog with Top Right (the default), Top
Left, Bottom Right and Bottom Left; the choice is saved across restarts. The
floating icon size is 16x16, 32x32, 48x48 (the default), 64x64, 128x128 or
256x256; the settings ring doubles in width at 128x128 and triples at 256x256.
The Opacity menu offers 10%, 20%, 30%, 40%, 50%, 60%, 70%, 80% and 90%.
"Reset Settings" returns to red, 6 px, 100% opacity, no shape fill, Arial Bold
24, a 60 px eraser and a 48x48 icon.
"Show Floating Icon (F9)" sits
directly below "Toggle Visibility" in the main menu. With "Save
annotations on exit" on (the default), the drawings are saved to
.I ~/.config/screenator-annotations.png
when Screenator quits and restored on the next start; the screen paper follows
its own setting. "Clear Screen" removes the saved drawing.
.br
A single, colourful Hints dialog appears when Screenator starts. It is a
750x550 scrollable column that summarises the common mouse and stylus controls
plus shape-editing tips, the global F9 shortcut and the drawing-mode and
screen-paper shortcuts in one place instead
of repeating them for every tool, and it emphasises that a long right click, or a
long press of the stylus second side button, opens the Scroll Mode menu to
choose Color or Thickness for scrolling. A "Never show hints again" option
beside the Close button is remembered across restarts. "Show Hints" in the menu opens the
dialog again, and Reset Settings keeps the choice.
.br
.SH KEYBOARD CONTROL
By default, Screenator grabs the "F9" key globally (this can be changed using
the "\-\-key" option), making it unavailable to other applications. Pressing
.B F9
shows or hides the floating icon at any time.
.B F8
selects the Pen tool,
.B F6
selects the Arrow tool and
.B F7
selects the Text tool. While a tool is active these switch immediately;
otherwise they honor the Tool Activation Delay.
.B F10
opens a centered quick tool menu with every tool and icon; use the arrow keys
to move and Enter to select. A tool chosen from this menu activates
immediately, ignoring the Tool Activation Delay. The old Gromit-MPX shortcuts
(Shift+F9, Ctrl+F9, Alt+F9, Shift+F8 and the old F8 undo) are no longer
grabbed.
.PP
While painting mode is active, the usual
.B Ctrl+Z
and
.B Ctrl+Shift+Z
shortcuts undo and redo. Pressing
.B Esc
exits the current tool, just like right-clicking outside a shape. These keys
are only grabbed while painting, so they keep their normal meaning in other
applications.
.SH STYLUS
.B IMPORTANT:
In your stylus configuration, set button 1 (near the tip) to Middle Click and
button 2 to Right Click.
The stylus tip draws like the left mouse button, using the selected line
width; pen pressure is ignored so it draws exactly like the mouse. The first
side button has two short-press settings chosen in the stylus menu: Color steps
the line colour (or the fill colour over the Paint Bucket), and Thickness steps
through the line thicknesses. A long press of the first side button activates
the eraser while it is held, also while the pen hovers above the tablet. A
short click of the second side button acts like Escape/right click, finishing
the shape being edited and keeping the tool active, or leaving painting mode
when no shape is being edited. A long press opens the "Button 1 Mode" menu with
Color and Thickness. With the Eraser tool, a short press of the first side
button steps through the eraser sizes and a long press of the second side
button does not open the menu. The hints dialog covers the stylus controls at
startup and can be reopened with "Show Hints".
.SH OPTIONS (STARTUP)
A short summary of the available command line arguments for starting Screenator;
see below for the options to control an already running Screenator process:
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-active
start Screenator and immediately activate it.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-debug
gives some debug output.
.TP
.B \-k <keysym>, \-\-key <keysym>
change the global key used to show or hide the floating icon. <keysym> can
e.g. be "F9", "F12", "Control_R" or "Print". To determine the keysym for
different keys you can use the \fBxev\fP(1) command. You can specify "none"
to prevent Screenator from grabbing a key.
.TP
.B \-K <keycode>, \-\-keycode <keycode>
change the floating-icon key by keycode instead of keysym.
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-opacity <value>
set the initial drawing opacity using a value between 0 and 1.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
show the Screenator version.
.SH OPTIONS (CONTROL)
A short summary of the available command line arguments to control an already
running Screenator process:
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-clear
clear the screen.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quit
cause the running Screenator process to quit.
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-toggle
toggle the grabbing of the pointer.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-visibility
toggle the visibility of the drawing.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-reload
reload the tool configuration.
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-redo
redo the last undone drawing stroke.
.TP
.B \-z, \-\-undo
undo the last drawing stroke.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP
Screenator uses this to determine which desktop environment it is running on.
.TP
.B XDG_CURRENT_SESSION
Screenator uses this to determine whether it is running under X11 or Wayland.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Directory to search for the user's configuration files, defaults to
.BI ~ /.config/ .
.TP
.B GDK_CORE_DEVICE_EVENTS
If set, GDK does not use the XInput extension and only reacts to core X input
events, which renders Screenator unusable.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/screenator.cfg
User tool configuration: defines pens and maps mouse buttons and modifiers to
them.
.TP
.I /etc/screenator/screenator.cfg
System-wide tool configuration, used when the user configuration does not
exist.
.TP
.I ~/.config/screenator.ini
Saved menu settings (line colour, thickness, opacity, shape fill, screen
paper, floating icon size, settings indicator visibility, whether annotations
are saved on exit, text font, math expression and range, and the selected
tool).
.TP
.I ~/.config/screenator-annotations.png
The drawing layer saved when Screenator quits, restored on the next start.
.SH NOTES
Screenator is X11-only. Under Wayland it runs through XWayland.
